# Code owners. @obviousthrowaway is the repository owner. If you publish your own copy,
# replace it with your account (a user or a team) before applying the main branch's
# ruleset with scripts/apply_repo_rules.sh. An owner GitHub cannot resolve owns nothing,
# so no file would then need a code owner's review.
#
# The ruleset (.github/rulesets/main.json) requires pull requests with zero approvals
# but "Require review from Code Owners", so:
#
# - a pull request that touches only the ownerless account records below needs no
#   review; the Registry gate, a required check, validates and spot-checks them, and
#   asks for the owner's approval of every high-impact change among them;
# - a pull request that touches any other file needs the owner's approval.
#
# The last matching pattern wins. In these patterns "*" never matches "/".

# Everything is the owner's: code, workflows, docs, the schema, CODEOWNERS itself.
* @obviousthrowaway

# Account records: registry/events/<event-id>/<platform>/<shard>.jsonl. No owner, so
# no code owner review. The Registry gate enforces the data policy instead.
/registry/events/*/*/*.jsonl

# Evidence files (registry/events/<event-id>/evidence/<platform>/<id_b64>.md) stay
# the owner's. This line comes after the records line, so it also wins for any file
# directly under an evidence/ folder.
/registry/events/*/evidence/ @obviousthrowaway

# The schema and the outage events file (every event's cutoff decides which accounts
# count as silent) stay the owner's.
/registry/schema.json @obviousthrowaway
/events.toml @obviousthrowaway

# The checks that judge registry pull requests and the rules themselves.
/.github/ @obviousthrowaway
/scripts/apply_repo_rules.sh @obviousthrowaway
/src/bot_farm_silence_tracker/registry/ @obviousthrowaway
